{:edb/db-before-t 9169 :edb/db-after-t 9170 :edb/tx-data [[13194139542482 50 #inst "2026-10-28T05:42:20.863Z" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186061921 1078 #edb/ref 17592186061923 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186061922 1080 "9\\operatorname{arccsc} y + C" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186061922 1080 "9arccscy + C" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186061924 1080 "9\\operatorname{arccot} y + C" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186061924 1080 "9arccoty + C" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186061927 1078 #edb/ref 17592186061929 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186062437 1078 #edb/ref 17592186062441 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186062449 1078 #edb/ref 17592186062454 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186062455 1078 #edb/ref 17592186062456 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186063138 1078 #edb/ref 17592186063143 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186063139 1080 "3\\operatorname{arcsec} x + 6x^{2} + C" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186063139 1080 "3arcsecx + 6x^{2} + C" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186063143 1080 "3\\operatorname{arcsec} x + 12x + C" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186063143 1080 "3arcsecx + 12x + C" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186063168 1078 #edb/ref 17592186063173 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186063390 1078 #edb/ref 17592186063395 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186064539 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =11\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186064539 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =11\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11arcsecx+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186064644 1074 "Using $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dy}\\arcsin y=\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int\\frac9{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}\\,\\mathrm dy=9\\arcsin y+C.\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on $-1<y<1$." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186064645 1074 "Using $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}\\arctan x=\\frac1{1+x^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int\\frac3{1+x^2}\\,\\mathrm dx=3\\arctan x+C.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186064730 1074 "The exponential function is its own derivative. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\int3e^x\\,\\mathrm dx=3e^x+C.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186064732 1074 "For $a>0$ and $a\\ne1$, $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}a^x=a^x\\ln a$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\int4\\cdot5^x\\,\\mathrm dx=\\frac{4\\cdot5^x}{\\ln5}+C.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186064733 1074 "Integrate each term:\n\n$$\n\\int(2^x-10e^x)\\,\\mathrm dx=\\frac{2^x}{\\ln2}-10e^x+C.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186064847 1074 "The derivative of $\\operatorname{arcsec}x$ is $\\frac1{|x|\\sqrt{x^2-1}}$ on $|x|>1$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\int\\left(\\frac3{|x|\\sqrt{x^2-1}}+12\\right)\\,\\mathrm dx=3\\operatorname{arcsec}x+12x+C.\n$$\n\nThe constant may be chosen independently on the intervals $(-\\infty,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty)$." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186064852 1074 "Rewrite the numerator as $x^2-4=(x^2+1)-5$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int\\frac{x^2-4}{x^2+1}\\,\\mathrm dx&=\\int\\left(1-\\frac5{x^2+1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\&=x-5\\arctan x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186064889 1074 "Integrate term by term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int(2e^x+4^x-2x)\\,\\mathrm dx&=2e^x+\\frac{4^x}{\\ln4}-x^2+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186069532 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186069532 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this family on a connected interval where $x^2+x+C>0$. This condition preserves the positive square-root branch used before raising both sides to a power." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186075106 1078 #edb/ref 17592186075111 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186077732 1078 #edb/ref 17592186077735 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186077738 1078 #edb/ref 17592186077739 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186077744 1078 #edb/ref 17592186077748 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186078020 1078 #edb/ref 17592186078022 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186078705 1074 "An inhibited decay equation is proportional to the difference between the limiting value and the current value. With limiting value $40$ and rate constant $20$, it is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}=20(40-y)=800-20y.\n$$\n\nFor $y>40$ this derivative is negative and approaches zero as $y$ approaches $40$." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186079159 1074 "Substitute $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ into the equation and divide by the nonzero factor $e^{\\lambda x}$:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+6=(\\lambda-2)(\\lambda-3)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\lambda=2$ or $\\lambda=3$." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186079160 1074 "The characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-5\\lambda-6=(\\lambda-6)(\\lambda+1).\n$$\n\nThus all solutions are linear combinations of $e^{6x}$ and $e^{-x}$. The option $e^{5x}$ is not such a solution: substitution gives\n\n$$\n(25-25-6)e^{5x}=-6e^{5x}\\ne0.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186079161 1074 "The exponential trial solution leads to\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2+9\\lambda+18=(\\lambda+6)(\\lambda+3)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\lambda=-6$ or $\\lambda=-3$." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186079207 1074 "The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-3\\lambda-10=(\\lambda-5)(\\lambda+2)=0.\n$$\n\nThe two distinct roots are $5$ and $-2$, so the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{-2x}+Be^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary real constants." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186084836 1078 #edb/ref 17592186084841 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186086586 1078 #edb/ref 17592186086588 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186086604 1078 #edb/ref 17592186086608 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186087143 1078 #edb/ref 17592186087148 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186087149 1078 #edb/ref 17592186087153 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186087179 1078 #edb/ref 17592186087184 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186087317 1078 #edb/ref 17592186087319 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186087458 1074 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}\n$$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186087458 1074 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}\n$$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186088017 1074 "The decay model has solution $N(t)=230000e^{-kt}$. Use the measurement after five years:\n\n$$\n23000=230000e^{-5k}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k=\\frac{\\ln10}{5}\\approx0.460517.\n$$\n\nThe continuous decay rate is approximately $46\\%$ per year, to the nearest percent." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186088322 1074 "Compare with $\\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1$. Here $a=\\sqrt{10}$ and $b=3$, with $a>b$. The lengths of the major and minor axes are therefore\n\n$$\n2a=2\\sqrt{10},\\qquad2b=6.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186088325 1074 "The graph crosses the $x$-axis at $\\pm3$ and the $y$-axis at $\\pm4$. Thus the horizontal and vertical semiaxes are $3$ and $4$, respectively. Its equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{3^2}+\\frac{y^2}{4^2}=1,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186088415 1074 "The initial depth is $D(0)=300$ feet. Solving $D^{\\prime}=0.01D$ gives\n\n$$\nD(t)=300e^{0.01t}.\n$$\n\nAfter six years,\n\n$$\nD(6)=300e^{0.06}\\approx318.550964\\text{ feet}.\n$$\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, the depth is $319$ feet." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186088416 1074 "The solution is $V(t)=4800e^{kt}$. Apply the value after three years:\n\n$$\n7200=4800e^{3k}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k=\\frac{\\ln(3/2)}3\\approx0.135155.\n$$\n\nThe continuous annual growth rate is $14\\%$, to the nearest percent." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186088421 1074 "The solution with the given initial price is\n\n$$\nP(t)=250000e^{0.0325t}.\n$$\n\nAfter $25$ years,\n\n$$\nP(25)=250000e^{0.8125}\\approx563383.6968.\n$$\n\nRounded to the nearest dollar, the price is $\\$563384$." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186088437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087275 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186088444 1074 "Set $z=y^2$, so $z^{\\prime}=2yy^{\\prime}$. Multiplying the ODE by $2y$ gives\n\n$$\nz^{\\prime}=x+\\frac{3z}{x}.\n$$\n\nOn an interval with $x\\ne0$, multiply $z^{\\prime}-3z/x=x$ by $x^{-3}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}(zx^{-3})=x^{-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating gives $zx^{-3}=-x^{-1}+K$, hence\n\n$$\ny^2=Kx^3-x^2.\n$$\n\nThe original ODE requires $x\\ne0$ and $y\\ne0$. This implicit expression covers both sign branches on intervals where $Kx^3-x^2>0$." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186088902 1064 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $\\color{red}a$ and a vertical radius of length $\\color{blue}b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{{\\color{red}a}^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{{\\color{blue}b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/92/9227839bbf9a01312c3014d336a88176eecd73aa934fcd4741c11271b1e0bea6.png)\n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the equation of the following ellipse. The horizontal radius has length $\\color{red}a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $\\color{blue}b=2.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe7dff90438e9571ae599a446e355776fbd36ed4746dd76e6633f3848e1f6bbc.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186088902 1064 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $\\color{red}a$ and a vertical radius of length $\\color{blue}b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{{\\color{red}a}^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{{\\color{blue}b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/92/9227839bbf9a01312c3014d336a88176eecd73aa934fcd4741c11271b1e0bea6.png)\n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the equation of the following ellipse. The horizontal radius has length $\\color{red}a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $\\color{blue}b=2.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe7dff90438e9571ae599a446e355776fbd36ed4746dd76e6633f3848e1f6bbc.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{{\\color{red}3}}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{{\\color{blue}2}}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186089243 1078 #edb/ref 17592186089246 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186090604 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090607 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186090610 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090612 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186092512 1074 "The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm df}{\\mathrm dx}=e^{3x}\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}(3x)=3e^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186092733 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090598 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186092734 1074 "Differentiate the outer exponential and multiply by the derivative of its exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=e^{x^4+1}\\cdot4x^3=4x^3e^{x^4+1}.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186092735 1074 "For a constant base, $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}a^{u(x)}=a^{u(x)}\\ln(a)u^{\\prime}(x)$. With $u(x)=6x^2-4$,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=3^{6x^2-4}\\ln(3)\\cdot12x.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186093806 1078 #edb/ref 17592186093807 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186094093 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094096 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186094681 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094684 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186095424 1078 #edb/ref 17592186095425 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186095472 1078 #edb/ref 17592186095474 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186095664 1078 #edb/ref 17592186095669 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186096972 1074 "Let $u=3x^5-4x^2$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=3u^2u^{\\prime}\\\\&=3(3x^5-4x^2)^2(15x^4-8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186097020 1074 "On an interval avoiding $x=1$, rearrange and integrate:\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}=-\\frac2{(x-1)^2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac2{x-1}+C.\n$$\n\nThe condition $y(2)=1$ gives $1=2+C$, so $C=-1$. Thus\n\n$$\ny=\\frac2{x-1}-1,\\qquad x>1.\n$$\n\nThe interval $(1,\\infty)$ is the maximal interval containing the initial point." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186097118 1074 "Rearrange as $f^{\\prime}(x)=-11x$ and integrate:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\int-11x\\,\\mathrm dx=-\\frac{11}{2}x^2+C.\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186097250 1074 "With $u=x^4+3x$, we have $f=u^2$ and $u^{\\prime}=4x^3+3$. Therefore\n\n$$\nf^{\\prime}(x)=2(x^4+3x)(4x^3+3).\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186097258 1074 "Write $y=(4x-3)^{1/2}$ and apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}=\\frac12(4x-3)^{-1/2}\\cdot4=\\frac2{\\sqrt{4x-3}}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative is defined for $x>3/4$." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186097290 1074 "Rearrange and integrate:\n\n$$\nf^{\\prime}(x)=-\\frac12e^x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(x)=-\\frac12e^x+C.\n$$\n\nUsing $f(0)=-1$ gives $-1=-1/2+C$, hence $C=-1/2$. Therefore\n\n$$\nf(x)=-\\frac12(e^x+1).\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186097300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095724 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186097301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095730 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186097302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095736 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186097807 1064 "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{′}(a) & =\\lim_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{′}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{′}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186097807 1064 "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{′}(a) & =\\lim_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{′}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{′}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186122996 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123001 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186125066 1074 "Inhibited growth is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. The capacity is $85$, so the model is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dR}{\\mathrm dt}=k(85-R),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nThe initial population $20$ determines the initial condition $R(0)=20$; it does not change the limiting capacity." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186149839 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149842 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186149844 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149846 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186149860 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149862 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186149865 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149867 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186149868 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149870 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186150173 1078 #edb/ref 17592186150177 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186151907 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186151907 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186152565 1071 "Given the set of functions $\\{x,\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.167em}2x\\}$ defined on $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186152565 1071 "Given the set of functions ${x,\\,{e}^{x},\\,2x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186152565 1074 "Recall that the set of functions $\\{{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)\\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\n2\\cdot x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186152565 1074 "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n2\\cdot x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186152566 1074 "The relation $k_1+k_2\\sin x=0$ must hold for every real $x$. At $x=0$ it gives $k_1=0$. At $x=\\pi/6$ it gives\n\n$$\nk_1+\\frac12k_2=0.\n$$\n\nCombining these equations gives $k_2=0$. Thus only the trivial coefficients work, so $1$ and $\\sin x$ are linearly independent." 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186152567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149849 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186152567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149852 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186152567 1071 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186152567 1071 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186152567 1074 "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186152567 1074 "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot