bmatrix}+{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 false] [17592186390978 1074 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{4(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+16\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(4)+0(48) \\\\ 0(4)+4(48)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 192\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542482 17592186576411 true] [17592186390978 1074 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+16\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 